  "6.4": {
    s: ["Explain why random sampling matters and identify biased procedures.", "Use the sampling distribution of the sample mean and the Central Limit Theorem.", "Calculate unbiased estimates of population mean and variance.", "Construct and interpret confidence intervals for a mean or large-sample proportion."],
    d: [["Population", "The full set about which an inference is made."], ["Sample", "A subset observed to learn about the population."], ["Sampling distribution", "The probability distribution of a statistic over repeated samples."], ["Standard error", "The standard deviation of a sampling distribution."], ["Confidence interval", "A range generated by a method that captures the true parameter at a stated long-run rate."]],
    p: [["Random samples and bias", "Every population member should have an appropriate chance of selection. Convenience, voluntary-response and incomplete-frame samples can be biased. Random numbers help choose units without personal judgement.", ["A large biased sample is still biased.", "Distinguish sampling variation from measurement error."]], ["Sample mean distribution", "If population mean is μ and variance σ², then E(X̄)=μ and Var(X̄)=σ²/n. X̄ is normal if the population is normal and approximately normal for large n by the CLT.", ["Standard error is σ/√n.", "Increasing n reduces standard error by the square-root law."]], ["Unbiased estimates", "Estimate μ by x̄. Estimate population variance with s²=Σ(x−x̄)²/(n−1), equivalently [Σx²−(Σx)²/n]/(n−1). The n−1 correction makes the estimating process unbiased.", ["Use the sample variance requested, not the descriptive variance with denominator n.", "Keep x̄ unrounded in the variance formula."]], ["Confidence intervals", "For known σ or a large sample, a confidence interval for μ is x̄±z*σ/√n, replacing σ by s for a large sample when needed. For a large-sample proportion p, use p̂±z*√[p̂(1−p̂)/n]. Interpret the confidence level as a property of the method.", ["90%, 95%, 99% use different central z-values.", "Give interval endpoints in context and sensible units."]]],
    f: [["Sample mean moments", "E(X̄)=μ; Var(X̄)=σ²/n"], ["Unbiased variance", "s²=[Σx²−(Σx)²/n]/(n−1)"], ["Mean confidence interval", "x̄±z*·σ/√n"], ["Proportion confidence interval", "p̂±z*√[p̂(1−p̂)/n]"]],
    r: [["What is the standard error of X̄?", "σ/√n."], ["Why is n−1 used in the sample variance?", "It corrects the systematic underestimation and makes the estimator unbiased."], ["What does a 95% confidence method mean?", "Across repeated samples, about 95% of intervals produced by the method contain the true parameter."], ["What happens to standard error if n is quadrupled?", "It is halved."]],
    t: ["Name the parameter and confidence level in the interpretation sentence.", "Check that a proportion interval stays sensible relative to 0 and 1."],
    m: ["Saying there is a 95% probability a fixed calculated interval contains a fixed parameter.", "Using σ/n instead of σ/√n.", "Using denominator n for the required unbiased sample-variance estimate."],
    rel: ["5.1", "5.5", "6.5"]
  },
